The Dow Jones Industrial Average slashed an opening 150-point drop to close sharply higher Friday, on the back of a pop in Apple Inc. (AAPL). Shares of Apple contributed the most to the price-weighted Dow after a report from CNBC late Thursday said Warren Buffett's Berkshire Hathaway Inc. (BRKA)(BRKA) acquired some 75 million additional shares (http://www.marketwatch.com/story/warren-buffetts-berkshire-hathaway-bought-75-million-apple-shares-in-first-quarter-2018-05-03) of the iPhone maker during the first quarter. Apple's stock closed at a record, up 3.9%, or $6.94, at $183.83, contributing about 48 points to the Dow, with a $1 move in any one of the blue-chip gauge's 30 components equating to a 6.89-point swing. The closed up 332 points, or 1.4%, at 24,262. Meanwhile, the S&P 500 index (http://www.marketwatch.com/story/us-stock-futures-tip-lower-in-countdown-to-jobs-data-2018-05-04) ended up about 1.3% at 2,663.42, while the Nasdaq Composite Index advanced by 1.7% at 7,209.62. Apple is the largest company by market value and tends to have an outsize influence on market-value weighted indexes like the Nasdaq and S&P 500.
-Mark DeCambre;
